w it into the area there's always  some traffic  or some organization out there who is  figuring out how to maneuver around  those obstacles this is probably  attributable to changes in smuggling  patterns and changes in organized crime  in Mexico Garcia and his team have spent  decades searching for border tunnels but  every time they fill one up a new better  tunnel emerges way back to TJ to Mexico  as the Sinaloa cartel has moved in to  Baja California their organization has  been credited or blamed with the  construction of some super tunnels I  mean very long very deep professionally  constructed tunnels we were essentially  playing whack-a-mole hit it here and  then another time would pop up and you'd  hit it there it was a decided approach  by all the partners to do a long-term  investigation and try to go after those  people responsible this reflects a  broader shift in the government's  overall strategy in the war on drugs to  take down kingpins and dismantle the  cartels they recently took down the  notorious el chapo guzman of the Sinaloa  cartel who happened to have an elaborate  series of escape tunnels underneath his  home but shirk says the strategy comes  with its own pitfalls was when the  government decided to take on drug  traffickers that the drug war became a  literal war and we've seen an enormous  amount of bloodshed in Mexico precisely  because of internal splits of these  organizations competition from other  organizations that has been a direct  result of government efforts to take out  top leadership how does the arrest of  someone like chapo guzman affect how we  do business it changes things in the  sense that there's a kingpin that's no  longer there but we're not so naive to  think that there isn't other people  there that can step right in chapo  guzman was really one of a number of  different leaders of that organization  that have held it together we've only  taken out part of the leadership's like  you're taking away one of the eyes but  the other eye is still there and so that  it's not a full decapitation of the  organization it's not even a lobotomy  they're trying to enforce a law  that is difficult if not impossible to  enforce because it doesn't have buy-in  from a very significant portion of our  population I think it's kind of like the  little boy at the stickiness thumb into  the you know leaking dike or the dam you  know at what point you just give up I  don't think that anybody that is truly  embracing law enforcement as a career  ever wants to give up garcia may think  of himself as the boy with his finger in  the dike patching a leak until backup  arrives with a more permanent fix to  avert a flood but there's another way to  think about America's 40-year drug war a  tiny tunnel appears you fill it then you  see a bigger one so you hire more people  to fill the bigger hole of course  there's eventually an even bigger tunnel  with ventilation professionals dug this  tunnel so you need a special team to  keep up with these professionals on the  other side and now it's just you and the  professionals you both know what you're  doing you both get a little better every  time the tunnels are getting bigger and  better but at least you're finding them  you are finding all of them right we  want to make it so unattractive to do  the type of work that they do that  they'll go somewhere else and do  something else  we know that's a long hard road and you  know we may never be successful in our  lifetimes but we have to continue to lay  that foundation now
 

